Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you would invoke the **`maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter`** helper to decide whether a synthetic rest parameter needs to be added to a function declaration that already contains a variadic type in its last parameter.

> **Important** – The helper mutates the `parameters` array *in place*.  
> After the call you can inspect the array (or the returned value) to see whether a new rest parameter was inserted.

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Import the helper from the namespace that the developer exposed.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const { maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter } = functions;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Prepare your data.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   • `funcDeclaration` – the raw declaration object (could be a string,
//     an AST node, etc.).  For the purpose of this demo we’ll just use a
//     placeholder string.
//   • `funcParameters` – the array of parameter names/types that belong to
//     the declaration.  This is the array that the helper will examine
//     and potentially mutate.
//
//   In a real scenario you would replace the placeholders with the actual
//   objects coming from your parser / compiler pipeline.
const funcDeclaration = `
/**
 * Example function that already has a variadic type.
 * @param {...number} nums
 */
function sum(...nums) { /* … */ }
`;

const funcParameters = ['...nums'];   // ← last element is already variadic

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Call the helper.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The helper signature expects two string arguments, but the description
// indicates that the second argument is actually the array itself.  The
// implementation will therefore treat it as the array to be inspected.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter({
  declaration: funcDeclaration,
  parameters: funcParameters
});

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Inspect the result.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// If the helper decided that a synthetic rest parameter is required, it
// will have pushed a new element onto `funcParameters`.  Otherwise the
// array remains unchanged.
console.log('Updated parameters:', funcParameters);
```

### What to look for

| Condition | Expected behaviour |
|-----------|--------------------|
| **Last parameter is already variadic** (`...args`) | **No** synthetic rest parameter is added. |
| **JSDoc contains `@variadic` but the last parameter is not variadic** | A synthetic rest parameter is appended. |
| **Neither condition is met** | No change. |

After running the snippet above, you can verify whether a new rest parameter was added by checking the length of `funcParameters` or by inspecting its contents. If the array still contains only `['...nums']`, the helper correctly determined that no synthetic rest parameter was necessary. If you see an additional element (e.g., `'...syntheticRest'`), then the helper added it because the JSDoc indicated a variadic requirement that wasn’t reflected in the original signature.

